                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_misaligned:    `<strong>That is the right diagnosis.</strong> The system rewards billable hours and tenure — exactly the staff-augmentation behaviour the new strategy is moving away from. More money in that structure buys more of the wrong thing. The reset is about <em>fit</em>, not the size of the cheque.`,
                    coach_r1_underpaid:     `<strong>“Just underpaid” is the across-the-board-raise trap.</strong> Lift base 6% to match Vienna and the money comes from a frozen €49.5M envelope — from where? You will have rewarded the leavers and the stayers identically, still on tenure. The competitor offers progression and outcome bonuses; you offer a slightly higher flat salary.`,
                    coach_r1_managers:      `<strong>Weak managers are a symptom, not the root.</strong> The exit interviews say “pay not linked to contribution” and “no growth path” — that is a reward-design problem the best manager in the world cannot coach around. Fix the system the managers have to work inside.`,
                    coach_r1_costignore:    `<strong>“A normal cost of the sector” is how the board tunes you out.</strong> Put a number on it: ~€4.3M/yr in regretted exits and a projected 3-point margin erosion within 12 months. A reset is a hard sell without the cost of inaction quantified.`,
                    coach_r1_costhours:     `<strong>Framing it as lost billable hours misreads the whole problem.</strong> You are about to move <em>away</em> from billing hours as the measure of value — so pricing the loss in hours anchors the board to the old logic you are trying to leave.`,
                    coach_r1_grandfather:   `<strong>Leaving the 11% gap untouched is not a neutral choice.</strong> Under EU pay-transparency rules employees can now see it. Designing a shiny new system on an inequitable base means you have not actually solved the trust problem — you have ratified it.`,
                    coach_r1_shallow:       `<strong>A shallow diagnosis will cost you in every later round.</strong> Without segmenting the workforce — who is at flight risk, who is under-market, where the gap sits — your redesign is a guess. The data you skip now is the data the works council and the CEO will ask for.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the strategy misalignment as the root, quantified the cost of inaction, and treated pay equity as a precondition rather than an afterthought. That is the foundation a redesign can actually stand on.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That is vertical fit.</strong> Team delivery quality, client outcomes, skill acquisition and margin reward the ownership-and-outcomes behaviour the product strategy needs — not the billable hours you are leaving behind. The variable pay now buys the behaviour the CEO is betting the company on.`,
                    coach_r2_billable:      `<strong>You put a bigger bonus on billable hours — the exact behaviour the strategy is moving away from.</strong> Your engineers will optimise for billable time, not the product outcomes that justify the reset. What did your incentive actually buy? More of the old model, paid for at a premium.`,
                    coach_r2_individual:    `<strong>Aggressive individual ranking will spike short-termism and break collaboration.</strong> Force-ranking a delivery organisation pits engineers against each other; the platform strategy needs the opposite — shared ownership and cross-team handoffs. You are designing the perverse incentive in from day one.`,
                    coach_r2_overvariable:  `<strong>Past ~22% variable you over-load risk onto staff.</strong> A move from 8% toward the 15–20% band changes risk-sharing and behaviour — but pushing too far, too fast, in a transition-economy pay culture reads as the company offloading its volatility onto people's pay packets. Bold is not the same as right.`,
                    coach_r2_timid:         `<strong>You barely moved off 8%.</strong> Peers run 18–22% variable for a reason — it signals what the organisation values and rewards. A token shift tells your engineers nothing actually changed, and the Vienna competitor still owns the progression story.`,
                    coach_r2_nodev:         `<strong>You funded the bonus pool and starved the development line.</strong> Exit interviews say the number-one reason people leave is “no growth path.” You funded the thing people do not leave for and cut the thing they do. How does that lower turnover?`,
                    coach_r2_acrosstheboard:`<strong>A 6% across-the-board raise breaks the frozen envelope.</strong> The board said redesign <em>within</em> €49.5M — that money has to come from somewhere, and a flat increase rewards leavers and stayers identically, still on tenure. You have spent the envelope without fixing the alignment.`,
                    coach_r3_grandfather:   `<strong>Grandfathering leaves the 11% gap exactly where the audit found it.</strong> Under EU pay-transparency rules employees can see it — and you have now layered a new system on top of an inequitable base. Which problem did you actually solve?`,
                    coach_r3_cosmetic:      `<strong>A new-hires-only fix leaves the headline gap intact for existing staff.</strong> The unexplained gap at engineer grades is what the audit measures and what employees can see. Cosmetic on the edges does not close it — and the senior engineers who feel under-paid know it.`,
                    coach_r3_collaboration: `<strong>Good — you re-weighted toward team delivery and client outcomes.</strong> That kills the hoarding and the cross-team handoff failures the individual scheme created, and it pulls the metric set back into line with the platform strategy. Horizontal fit restored: the levers now reinforce each other.`,
                    coach_r3_double:        `<strong>Doubling down on individual bonuses pours fuel on the fire.</strong> The competition you are rewarding is exactly what made knowledge-sharing collapse and lost the flagship account on a handoff nobody owned. Your reward design is now actively breaking the collaboration your strategy needs.`,
                    coach_r3_behaviourignore:`<strong>“People are just trying harder” misreads the side-effect.</strong> Internal competition that collapses knowledge-sharing and drops client handoffs is not effort — it is the perverse incentive your design created. Left alone, it compounds into more lost accounts and more exits.`,
                    coach_r3_leak:          `<strong>The works council found out from a leaked spreadsheet.</strong> However elegant the design, 130 people are worse off on day one with no transition and no voice — you may be legally and contractually unable to implement it. A reward change people did not see coming is a reward change they reject.`,
                    coach_r3_announce:      `<strong>A clean day-one announcement with no transition still lands hard.</strong> 130 people are worse off immediately and the works council had no say. Procedural fairness — voice, a transition period, a clear who-gains-who-loses — is what makes a sound design actually implementable.`,
                    coach_r3_fair:          `<strong>That is procedural fairness done right.</strong> Early works-council engagement, a transition period for those worse off, and a clear explanation of who gains and who loses turns a contested change into one people can accept. Even those who lose stay when the process is fair.`,
                    coach_r4_fit:           `<strong>Vertical and horizontal fit is the philosophy that holds.</strong> Pay, metrics and development reinforcing each other and the strategy, on an equitable base, is exactly the system Zupan's strategic-HRM lens prescribes — and the one the four KPIs reward.`,
                    coach_r4_pay:           `<strong>“Pay more and people stay” is the trap the whole reset was built to escape.</strong> Cash in the wrong structure rewards tenure and bills, not the behaviour the strategy needs — and inside a frozen envelope, more base means less for the variable and development that actually move retention.`,
                    coach_r4_control:       `<strong>“Reward is a cost to minimise” is the public-sector legacy you were asked to leave behind.</strong> The cheapest compliant system is exactly the flat, tenure-weighted culture that produced 21% turnover. Minimising spend is not the goal; aligning it is.`,
                    coach_r4_cost:          `<strong>Leading with “we held the envelope” answers a question the CEO did not ask.</strong> Holding budget is the constraint, not the achievement. The board wants to know whether reward now fits the strategy and lifts engagement — the envelope was always frozen.`,
                    coach_r4_onekpi:        `<strong>Pointing at the single best number is how a board stops trusting an HR director.</strong> They can see all four KPIs. Lead with the favourable one and downplay the rest, and they will assume the rest is worse than it is.`,
                    coach_r4_own:           `<strong>That owns the trade-off honestly.</strong> Naming what you re-mixed out of legacy base, into variable and development, who is worse off, and the transition that lands it fairly is exactly what a CEO trusts — a deliberate system, not a lucky number.`,
                    coach_r4_dodge:         `<strong>Dodging the trade-off invites the board to assume the worst.</strong> Inside a frozen envelope every euro added came from somewhere; refusing to name it reads as either not knowing or not wanting to say. Neither builds confidence in the reset.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Blaming Finance for the frozen envelope misses the point.</strong> The constraint was the test — a reset funded by re-mixing, not expanding. “A bigger budget would have fixed it” is precisely the answer the board designed the constraint to rule out.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
