                    insight_mastered:       `Reset mastered: you ran the genuine reward reset — a strategy-aligned metric set, variable pay in the peer band, a funded development line, and pay equity corrected at the base — all inside the frozen €49.5M envelope. Engagement rose toward the benchmark, turnover fell, the gap closed, and reward now fits the product strategy. Vertical and horizontal fit, restored.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `Envelope broken: you funded the reset by expanding spend rather than re-mixing it. The board's one hard constraint was €49.5M, and a reset that needs more money is not a reset — it is the across-the-board raise that rewards leavers and stayers alike and fixes neither alignment nor growth.`,
                    insight_metrics:        `Wrong metrics: your variable pay rewards behaviour the strategy is moving away from. Pay-for-performance drives whatever it measures — bill hours and you get hours. The incentive bought more of the old staff-augmentation model at a premium, the opposite of the product-and-platform shift the CEO is betting on.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed reset: some of the design landed, but the metric set, equity correction, development and change management did not fully reinforce each other. Enough seams remain for engagement and retention to lag and for the board to question whether the reward system actually fits the strategy.`,
                    insight_equity:         `Inequity entrenched: the 11% gap was never corrected at the base. Under EU pay-transparency rules employees can see it, so you have layered a new system on an inequitable foundation — a legal exposure and a trust problem that no incentive design can paper over.`,
                    insight_perverse:       `Perverse incentive live: the individual-bonus design that sparked internal competition was never fixed. Knowledge-sharing and cross-team handoffs — the collaboration your platform strategy depends on — keep breaking, and client accounts stay at risk.`,
                    insight_nodev:          `Development starved: you under-funded the talent-development line. The number-one reason engineers leave is “no growth path,” so cutting development to protect bonus cash funded the thing people do not leave for and starved the thing they do.`,
                    insight_changemgmt:     `Change mismanaged: you altered pay without a real transition or works-council engagement. A sound design that 130 people did not see coming and could not contest is a design they reject — and one you may be legally unable to implement.`,
                    insight_engagement_lag: `Aligned but not yet felt: the system fits the strategy and people are staying, but engagement is still short of the benchmark. Reward change moves perception slowly — the design is right, and the engagement gap is the lag, not a flaw.`,

                    hint_overbudget:        `You have broken the frozen €49.5M envelope. The reset must be funded by re-mixing existing pay, not expanding it — pull base back and reallocate to variable and development.`,
                    hint_metrics:           `Your variable pay rewards billable hours or individual ranking — the behaviour the strategy is leaving behind. Re-weight the metric set toward team delivery, client outcomes and skill before the next round.`,
                    hint_equity:            `The 11% pay gap is still open and now visible under EU rules. Correct it at the base — equal pay for equal work, on a funded phased plan — or it undermines the whole reset.`,
                    hint_perverse:          `An individual-bonus side-effect is breaking collaboration and risking client accounts. Re-weight toward team and client-outcome metrics to restore horizontal fit.`,
